     Jukka periodically recommends one way to avoid this risk:
first thing after unpacking the sources, modify the Makefile to
comment out the `install' target.  Another way to handle it is
to avoid using the default directory structure; make your own
directory name which incorporates nethack's version number in
the path.  (That's what I do, and I've kept the old playgrounds
for most of the versions going back to 3.1.3; one or two might
be missing and the ones older than that used the default path
and got overwritten).  It's easy enough to copy the score file
after setting up a new version.)  But this method won't help if
you're rebuilding the current version rather than a new one.


